`ca::trace::symbolizer`: turns the addresses of a `ca::trace::backtrace` (layer 3) into function
names, source files and lines, reading the running executable's own symbol tables and DWARF
debug information, without libc or external tools.

```c++
#include "ca/symbolizer.hpp"
#include "ca/trace.hpp"

[[gnu::noinline]] static auto where(void) noexcept -> ca::trace::backtrace {
	return ca::trace::backtrace::capture();
}

auto main(void) -> int {
	const ca::trace::backtrace here = where();
	auto symbols = ca::trace::symbolizer::make();
	if (!symbols)
		return 1;
	ca::trace::location found[ca::trace::location::SCOPES];
	const ca::usz count = symbols->lookup(here.addresses()[0], found);
	if (count == 0U || !found[count - 1U].function().find(ca::sv::str{"where"}).holds())
		return 2;
	return symbols->cleanup() ? 0 : 3;
}
```

`lookup(address, out)` writes the functions an address is in, innermost first: the functions the
compiler inlined there, then the function they were inlined into, which also carries the offset
from its start. Printed with `{}`, `symbols.of(backtrace.addresses())` reads:

```text
    #0 ca::trap (/src/lib/1-primitives/debug/trap.hpp:23) inlined
       deepest+0x1e (/src/test.cpp:8)
    #1 middle+0x9 (/src/test.cpp:14)
    #2 0x7f3c2a42a1c9
```

| Built with | Names | File and line | Inlined functions |
|---|---|---|---|
| `-g` | from DWARF, qualified (`ca::trap`) | yes | yes |
| no `-g` | from the symbol table, mangled (`_ZL7deepestv`) | no | no |
| stripped | none | no | no |

DWARF 4 and 5 are read (clang and gcc defaults), including indexed strings, addresses and range
lists; compressed debug sections (`-gz`) and split DWARF (`-gsplit-dwarf`) are not. Names are not
demangled: DWARF names need no demangling, symbol-table names stay mangled. Shared objects ca's
own loader (`ca::dl`) holds when make() runs are read as well, from their files; addresses in
libraries libc's loader mapped (libc itself) are not found and print as they are, and so are
those of libraries loaded after make().

make() maps the whole executable read-only, and each of those shared objects; names, files and
lines borrow from those mappings until cleanup(), even after a library is released. A lookup scans the unit holding the address entry by entry, so it is for reports, not
hot paths.
